issue.body:
### Which @angular/* package(s) are relevant/releated to the feature request?

elements, router

### Description

When using `@angular/elements` in combination with `@angular/router`, there is a problem with the router: when the custom elements is removed/destroyed (`disconnectedCallback()` or `ngOnDestroy()`), the `Router` instance remains active and is still listening for route changes.

You *could* call `this.router.dispose()` in the `ngOnDestroy()` of the `AppComponent`, but then the router is no longer usable when the `AppComponent` custom element is created again.

### Proposed solution

I would propose a `reset()` method on the router that changes the internal state of the router back to when it was first created. So the same state as just before you call `this.router.initialNavigation()`.

### Alternatives considered

This hack seems to work:

```typescript
ngOnInit(): void {
    const router = this.router as any;
    router.navigated = false;
    router.navigationId = 0;
    router.lastSuccessfulId = -1;

    this.router.initialNavigation();
  }

  ngOnDestroy(): void {
    const router = this.router as any;
    if (router.locationSubscription) {
      router.locationSubscription.unsubscribe();
      router.locationSubscription = undefined;
    }
  }

```

body:
After further consideration, I'm going to close this. The `RouterOutlet` is not made to support multiple registered instances for the same outlet name, and this is what's happening. Baking in a "fix" for these out of order register/deregister operations would just be a hack for an unsupported use-case. 

Instead, you should either ensure that you aren't creating a second outlet before the first is destroyed, or create a custom router outlet implementation that can handle this. For example, copy-paste the current implementation and change the `ngOnDestroy` to 

```
  ngOnDestroy(): void {
    // Ensure that the registered outlet is this one before removing it on the context.
    if (this.parentContexts.getContext(this.name)?.outlet === this) {
      this.parentContexts.onChildOutletDestroyed(this.name);
    }
  }
```

Yet another option would be to make the custom implementation actually support multiple `RouterOutlet` instances as described in: https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/31587#issuecomment-660378081
